5. Metamorph: what does "sandboxed validation" actually prove?

  validate_proposal_sandboxed copies data_dir to a temp dir, writes the payload
  into the copy, then runs a subprocess with cwd=repo root and returns
  test_passed = (returncode == 0). The test that covers it passes
  test_command=[python, "-c", "import sys; sys.exit(0)"], i.e. a command that
  cannot read anything and cannot fail.

  $ PYTHONPATH=novacore/src python f3-14-probes.py
  ### PROBE C: sandboxed validation of a proposal with unusable content
  test_passed: True
  returncode:  0
    child said: child_env_mentions_sandbox=False
    child said: child_cwd=C:\Users\surya\Documents\nova-f3-scratch
    child said: child_cwd_has_data_dir=False
    child said: child_can_read_target=False
  real data dir untouched: {"ok": true}

  A payload of "THIS IS NOT JSON AT ALL" is reported as validated, because the
  child process never sees the sandbox: no path in the environment, cwd is the
  repo root, and data/skills/x.json is not reachable from there. The only thing
  test_passed proves is "the novacore suite is green", which is true whether or
  not the payload is garbage. The real data dir is untouched, which is the one
  part of the claim that does hold.

  F3.14-F2 (finding): SandboxedEvidence.test_passed is not evidence about the
  proposal.

